#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197960 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "500d2422-474b-55a3-8fe7-05799ba578af" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Rectangular Solid"}] :question/id #uuid "5ff4ed47-dffe-534e-baf9-41bded65d457" :question/math-academy-id "e-12385" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 0, \\: 0, \\: 1 + xyz \\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4162/Source/Images/cac638a0e26e72df4bdfca80611d5bba.png)" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1+xyz\\rangle\n$$\n\nis non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4162/Source/Images/af13f1982951f06cd09e8d9249599d81.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 1$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$. The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n$\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle$.\nUsing the fact that $z = 1$ on $S_1$, we have\n$F \\cdot n_{1}|= ⟨0, 0, 1 + xyz⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0, 1⟩; = ⟨0, 0, 1 + xy⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0, 1⟩; = 1 + xy$.\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ as follows:\n$∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS|= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot n_{1}dS; = ∬_(S_{1})1 + xydS; = ∬_(D)1 + xydA; = ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{1}1 + xydxdy; = ∫_{0}^{1}[x + \\frac{1}{2}x^{2}y]_{x = 0}^{x = 1}dy; = ∫_{0}^{1}1 + \\frac{1}{2}ydy; = [y + \\frac{1}{4}y^{2}]_{0}^{1}; = \\frac{5}{4}$\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1$. The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_2$ is\n$\\mathbf n_2 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, -1\\rangle$.\nUsing the fact that $z = 0$ on $S_2$, we have\n$F \\cdot n_{2}|= ⟨0, 0, 1 + xyz⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0,-1⟩; = ⟨0, 0, 1⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0,-1⟩; =-1$.\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_2$ as follows:\n$∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS_{2}|= ∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot n_{2}dS; = ∬_(S_{2}) - 1dS; = ∬_(D) - 1dA; = - ∬_(D)dA; =-Area(D); = - (1 - 0)(1 - 0); =-1$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot ndS + ∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{4} + (-1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197961 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "315e5799-4bcc-5f2d-b30b-6a3c945e1589" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "06cccfa3-4b70-5e67-9e9f-9f5c83b319bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-12386" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 2x,\\: 2y+xz,\\: 2z-xy\\rangle$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4162/Source/Images/4b1c5dcc75491a1b343943157f8828e0.png)" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O$, the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\langle x, y, z\\rangle}{r}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\langle x, y, z\\rangle\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2+z^2=1\n$$\n\non $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF \\cdot n &= ⟨2x, 2y + xz, 2z - xy⟩ \\cdot ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= 2x^{2} + (2y + xz) \\cdot y + (2z - xy) \\cdot z \\\\\n&= 2x^{2} + 2y^{2} + xyz + 2z^{2} - xyz \\\\\n&= 2(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S)2dS \\\\\n&= 2∬_(S)dS \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot Area(S) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 4π \\cdot 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 8π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197974 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d07bcae4-cae8-5eb0-a93f-798ba20485a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Cylinder"}] :question/id #uuid "8d046f27-886d-5342-8868-e2c3ae731fa5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12453" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4162/Source/Images/0b8c76a27f89a5bf55914475ba7b8324.png)" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$, and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle\n$$\n\nis non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4162/Source/Images/f750ea73c79c9c2eddfa45ef88626057.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 4$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1$. The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n$\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle$.\nUsing the fact that $z = 4$ on $S_1$, we have\n$F \\cdot n_{1}|= ⟨0, 0, z\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0, 1⟩; = ⟨0, 0, 4\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0, 1⟩; = 4\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}$.\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ is given by\n$∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS|= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot n_{1}dS; = ∬_(S_{1})4\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dS; = 4∬_(D)\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dA$.\n\nApplying a change of variables\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\to (r,\\theta), \\textrm d A = r\\,\\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4∬_(D)\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}dA &= 4∬_(D)r \\cdot rdrdθ \\\\\n&= 4∫_{0}^{2π}∫_{0}^{1}r^{2}drdθ \\\\\n&= 4∫_{0}^{2π}dθ∫_{0}^{1}r^{2}dr \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 2π \\cdot [\\frac{1}{3}r^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 8π(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{8π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1$. Therefore, for every point on $S_2$, we have\n$F(x,y,0) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0 \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0\\rangle$.\nTherefore,\n$∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot ndS|= ∬_(S_{2})0 \\cdot ndS; = ∬_(S_{2})0dS; = 0$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S)F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot ndS + ∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot ndS \\\\\n&= \\frac{8π}{3} + 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{8π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "500d2422-474b-55a3-8fe7-05799ba578af" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Rectangular Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c92f6538-7b16-5ae6-956f-7222feae3a5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194070} {:answer/id #uuid "f5545bb8-4794-53db-8215-f7eda3020a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194071} {:answer/id #uuid "401c08d4-2de4-533d-8a7b-6a0c449f3b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194072} {:answer/id #uuid "57fffa5e-e7a8-597f-8aec-bcc2a6d82424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194073} {:answer/id #uuid "7882f132-5bd9-5109-b690-8eb3ee1f382c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7882f132-5bd9-5109-b690-8eb3ee1f382c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194074} :answer-field/id #uuid "24ffc3dd-c163-5c41-a448-a318e098e492" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194069}] :question/id #uuid "300078ae-d2f8-55e0-bd83-9b0b9c3615b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161162" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4162/Source/Images/q-161162.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨0, 0, 2x + yz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d07bcae4-cae8-5eb0-a93f-798ba20485a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82a68475-f1e3-5976-abba-f10554b133